ligious ideology and architectural innovation in Japanese temple design."}, {"sourceSlug": "unesco-world-heritage-list", "sourceName": "UNESCO World Heritage List", "verb": "CANONIZES", "targetSlug": "byd-in-temple", "targetName": "Byōdō-in Temple", "context": "Byōdō-in's designation as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1994 canonizes its cultural and historical significance on a global scale."}], "places": [{"name": "Uji, Japan", "role": "Location of Byōdō-in"}, {"name": "Kyoto, Japan", "role": "Cultural Center"}, {"name": "Nara, Japan", "role": "Historical Influence"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Byōdō-in Temple", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "byōdō-in-temple"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q61094", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/By%C5%8Dd%C5%8D-in"], "tags": ["Japan", "Buddhist temple", "Asia", "Temples & Shrines", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Byōdō-in Temple contributed to the historical record of the Medieval period. Key lasting impacts include: Shaped institutional landscape and community development. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Byōdō-in served as a vital center for Pure Land Buddhism, influencing Japanese religious practices and architectural styles, thus shaping the cultural landscape of Japan for centuries.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-05T12:59:55Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#28741465321", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Byōdō-in  is a Buddhist temple in the city of Uji in Kyoto Prefecture, Japan, built in the late Heian period. It is jointly a temple of the Jōdo-shū and Tendai-shū sects.", "newValue": "Byōdō-in Temple, located in Uji, Kyoto Prefecture, Japan, is a seminal Buddhist temple established in 1052 during the late Heian period. As a notable example of Pure Land Buddhism, it reflects the religious and cultural developments of medieval Japan. The temple was commissioned by Fujiwara no Yorimichi, a powerful figure in the Heian court, whose influence helped shape Japan's religious landscape. Byōdō-in not only serves as a religious institution but also as a symbol of the era's architectural sophistication and aesthetic ideals, encapsulating the harmony between nature and human-made structures.

The temple's construction was part of a broader movement during the Heian period that emphasized the Pure Land sect's teachings, which sought to make Buddhism accessible to the common people. Byōdō-in features the Phoenix Hall, or Hōō-dō, a stunning architectural masterpiece that houses a statue of Amida Buddha, illustrating the artistic and spiritual aspirations of the time. The temple's establishment coincided with increasing interactions between the aristocracy and the Buddhist clergy, facilitating a symbiotic relationship that would influence Japanese culture for centuries.

Byōdō-in's legacy extends far beyond its religious function; it has been designated a UNESCO World Heritage Site and continues to attract scholars, pilgrims, and tourists alike. Its architectural style has influenced subsequent temple designs throughout Japan, contributing to a distinct cultural identity. The temple's portrayal in art and literature serves as a testament to its enduring significance, making it a symbol of Japanese heritage."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-05T12:59:55Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#28741465321", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": ["Religious community formation and worship"], "newValue": ["The rise of Pure Land Buddhism during the Heian period created a demand for accessible religious practices for the aristocracy and common people.", "The political power of the Fujiwara clan enabled the construction of significant religious structures as symbols of their wealth and influence.", "The cultural flourishing of the Heian period fostered artistic achievements, leading to unique architectural styles reflected in Byōdō-in's design."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-05T12:59:55Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#28741465321", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": ["Shaped institutional landscape and community development"], "newValue": ["Byōdō-in established a template for Buddhist temple architecture that influenced subsequent designs throughout Japan.", "The temple contributed to the spread of Pure Land Buddhism, shaping the spiritual landscape of medieval Japan and beyond.", "Byōdō-in became a UNESCO World Heritage Site, enhancing its status as a cultural and historical landmark in Japan and attracting global attention."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-05T12:59:55Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#28741465321", "field": "detailsJson.relationships", "oldValue": [{"sourceSlug": "byd-in-temple", "sourceName": "Byōdō-in Temple", "verb": "OCCURS_DURING", "targetSlug": "era-high-medieval", "targetName": "High Medieval", "context": "Byōdō-